/target
**/*.rs.bk

# dev-meta — managed only in the violet_sw development monorepo
LOOPROMPT.md
CLAUDE.md

# generated simulation output
*.vcd

# Workload corpus (crates/corpus-runner · docs/study/03-workload-corpus.md).
#
# Two things live under bench/ and they are treated oppositely.
#
#   THIRD-PARTY RTL is never redistributed — not committed, not packaged. Each design
#   keeps its upstream licence in place and is cloned at a PINNED SHA into
#   bench/<name>/src by `corpus-runner fetch --run`.
#
#   THE HARNESS is committed: testbenches, file lists, firmware images and RUN.md are
#   work product of this repository. Excluding them was a real defect — a pinned SHA
#   reconstructs the upstream RTL but not the thing that produced the digest, so the
#   corpus was reproducible on exactly one machine and reported eight regressions
#   anywhere else.
/bench/**
!/bench/**/
# ...but never descend into a clone or a build directory.
/bench/*/src/
/bench/*/obj_dir*/
# The harness, by extension. Anything not listed here stays ignored, so a stray
# binary or scratch probe cannot be committed by accident.
!/bench/*/tb*.v
!/bench/*/tb*.sv
!/bench/*/files.txt
!/bench/*/RUN.md
!/bench/README.md
!/bench/*/README.md
!/bench/*/run.sh
!/bench/*/prepare.sh
!/bench/*/*.py
# ibex is NOT a corpus workload — iverilog 13 cannot parse it, so there is no oracle
# (ROADMAP §3 ⑤). The local tree is kept for reproduction; none of it is committed.
/bench/ibex/
# NOT firmware images. `blinky.hex` is byte-identical to serv's own sw/blinky.hex and
# `prog.hex` is extracted from biriscv's test.elf — both are third-party content, so
# they are fetched or regenerated (bench/<name>/prepare.sh), never committed.
# bench/keccak is OUR OWN RTL (see its README) and ships whole.
!/bench/keccak/*.sv
!/bench/keccak/*.py
